=========================================================
TEMPLATE METHOD DESIGN PATTERN
INTERVIEW NOTES (CLEAN & FINAL)
=========================================================

1. What is Template Method Pattern?
----------------------------------
Template Method is a Behavioral Design Pattern.

It defines the skeleton (template) of a workflow in a base class,
while allowing derived classes to implement or customize specific steps
without changing the overall workflow.

Definition:
"Defines the skeleton of a workflow in a base class and lets subclasses
override specific steps without changing the workflow."

---------------------------------------------------------

2. Why do we need Template Method?
----------------------------------

Problem:

Suppose we have Tea and Coffee.

Making Tea:
Boil Water
Add Tea Leaves
Pour into Cup
Add Sugar

Making Coffee:
Boil Water
Add Coffee Powder
Pour into Cup
Add Sugar

Notice:

✔ Boil Water → Same
✔ Pour into Cup → Same
✔ Add Sugar → Same

Only one step changes:
Tea Leaves / Coffee Powder

Without Template Method:

Duplicate code in every class.

Problems:

❌ Code duplication
❌ Difficult to maintain
❌ Common workflow repeated
❌ Violates DRY principle

---------------------------------------------------------

3. Solution
-----------

Move the common workflow into a base class.

Allow subclasses to customize only the changing steps.

                Beverage
                    ▲
          -------------------
          |                 |
         Tea            Coffee

---------------------------------------------------------

4. Real-Life Examples
---------------------

✔ Tea / Coffee preparation
✔ Report Generation
✔ Data Parsing
✔ File Processing
✔ Game Engine Loop
✔ Build Pipelines
✔ Document Export (PDF, Excel)
✔ Payment Processing Workflow

---------------------------------------------------------

5. Components
-------------

1. Abstract Base Class
- Defines the template method
- Implements common steps
- Declares abstract/customizable steps

2. Concrete Class
- Implements variable steps

---------------------------------------------------------

6. Structure
------------

          Base Class

templateMethod()

↓

step1()        (implemented)

↓

step2()        (virtual / abstract)

↓

step3()        (implemented)

↓

step4()        (virtual / abstract)

---------------------------------------------------------

7. Workflow
-----------

Client

↓

Calls templateMethod()

↓

Base class executes the workflow

↓

Calls overridden methods when needed

↓

Workflow completes

---------------------------------------------------------

8. Advantages
-------------

✔ Eliminates duplicate code

✔ Common workflow defined once

✔ Easy to extend

✔ Follows Open/Closed Principle

✔ Enforces a fixed sequence of steps

✔ Better code reuse

---------------------------------------------------------

9. Disadvantages
----------------

✘ Uses inheritance

✘ Less flexible than composition

✘ Deep inheritance can become difficult to maintain

---------------------------------------------------------

10. SOLID Principles Used
-------------------------

✔ Open/Closed Principle

Add new subclasses without modifying the base class.

✔ Single Responsibility Principle

Base class controls the workflow.
Derived classes implement specific behavior.

---------------------------------------------------------

11. Hook Methods
----------------

A Hook is an optional method that subclasses may override.

Example:

virtual bool addSugar()
{
    return true;
}

Tea may override:

bool addSugar() override
{
    return false;
}

---------------------------------------------------------

12. Common Interview Questions
------------------------------

Q. What problem does Template Method solve?

Answer:
It removes duplicate code by defining the common workflow once and
allowing subclasses to customize only selected steps.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q. Where is the workflow defined?

Answer:
In the base class.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q. Which methods are overridden?

Answer:
Only the variable steps.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q. Why is it called Template Method?

Answer:
Because the base class provides a template (fixed sequence of steps)
that every subclass follows.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q. Which design principle does it follow?

Answer:

Open/Closed Principle

---------------------------------------------------------

Q. What is a Hook Method?

Answer:
An optional method with a default implementation that subclasses
may override if needed.

---------------------------------------------------------

13. Strategy vs Template Method
-------------------------------

Strategy
--------
Uses Composition

Chooses one implementation at runtime.

Behavior can change dynamically.

Example:
Payment Method

Template Method
---------------
Uses Inheritance

Workflow is fixed.

Only certain steps are overridden.

Example:
Tea / Coffee preparation

---------------------------------------------------------

14. Factory Method vs Template Method
-------------------------------------

Factory Method
--------------
Creates objects.

Template Method
---------------
Defines the workflow.

---------------------------------------------------------

15. One-line Interview Answer
-----------------------------

Template Method is a behavioral design pattern that defines the skeleton
of a workflow in a base class while allowing subclasses to customize
specific steps without changing the overall workflow.

---------------------------------------------------------

